C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a doctrinal position and defends it from the whole canon. It is not a neutral survey of how the bride has been read, and it does not report each reading and leave the choice to the reader. It says what the author believes the passages teach, sets out the evidence from the law and the prophets to the Revelation, and answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ own terms."),
("p", "The position is this. In Scripture the bride is Israel. The LORD betrothed Israel to himself, charged her with unfaithfulness, put her away, and promised to take her again as his wife for ever (Hosea 2:14–23; Isaiah 54:1–8; 62:1–5; Jeremiah 3; Ezekiel 16). The Bridegroom of the Gospels is that same LORD come in the Messiah. The marriage of the Lamb in Revelation 19 and “the bride, the Lamb’s wife” of Revelation 21 complete that promise to that wife. The Body of Christ is not the bride. The Body has figures of its own, and when Paul borrows the pattern of marriage he teaches the Body how to love, how to be faithful and how to be holy. He does not give it the wife’s name."),
("p", "Many readers have been taught otherwise. Some are told that the church is the bride of Christ and that this is the plain sense of every bridal text. Some are told that Israel was set aside for her unfaithfulness and that the church now holds her place. Some are told that the bride is the church because Paul says he espoused the Corinthians to one husband, or because he calls the union of Christ and the church a great mystery. Each of these answers is held by serious and sincere teachers. This volume gives each a fair hearing, describes each in the terms its holders use, and says plainly where the text does not allow it to stand."),
("sub", "How the volume proceeds"),
("p", "The Thesis section states the position in six claims and the passages each rests on. Chapter 1 reads the title of husband that the LORD takes for himself, and Chapters 2 to 4 follow the wife through Hosea, Isaiah, Jeremiah and Ezekiel, ending with the covenant the marriage rests on. Chapter 5 reads Psalm 45, the king’s wedding song. Chapter 6 reads the Bridegroom in the Gospels. Chapter 7 reads the marriage of the Lamb in Revelation 19, and Chapter 8 reads the bride, the Lamb’s wife, in Revelation 21 and 22. Chapter 9 sets the harlot and the bride side by side and follows the voice of the bridegroom and the bride from Jeremiah to the last chapter of the book. Chapter 10 reads the three places where Paul uses the figure of marriage, and Chapter 11 gives the reasons the Body is not the bride. Chapter 12 asks who the guests at the supper are. Chapter 13 answers the objections, Chapter 14 walks through the key clauses one by one, and Chapter 15 sets out what the position permits a reader to say. A short conclusion restates the whole, and the appendices give the bridal thread at a glance, the parallels, the names of the wife, questions for study, a list of terms, and the key passages in full."),
("sub", "Scripture and sources"),
("p", "Scripture is quoted from the King James Version throughout, and every quotation has been checked against that text. The phrase “the Bride of Christ” does not stand in that version, and this volume uses the words the text uses. Where this volume names a reading held by a tradition, the reading is described fairly in the volume’s own words. Where a writer’s words could not be read in a full text, no quotation marks are used, and a view is attributed to a writer only where his words state it."),

("h1", "Thesis and the Passages It Rests On"),
("p", "The position can be put in six claims. Each is argued in the chapters that follow, and the passages beside it are the ones it chiefly rests on. A reader who disagrees with a claim is invited to go to those passages first."),
("ctbl", "The six claims, and what each rests on.", [
    ("Claim", "Rests chiefly on"),
    ("1. The LORD is the husband of Israel, and Israel is his wife: betrothed, unfaithful, put away and promised restoration.", "Isaiah 54:5; Jeremiah 2:2; 3:1–14; Hosea 2:2, 19–20; Ezekiel 16:8, 32"),
    ("2. The wife is the same wife throughout. No other people is named to take her place.", "Isaiah 50:1; 54:6–8; Jeremiah 3:14; Ezekiel 16:60–63; Hosea 3:3–5"),
    ("3. The Bridegroom of the Gospels is the LORD who spoke through the prophets, and the friend of the bridegroom is not the bride.", "John 3:29; Matthew 9:15; 22:1–14; 25:1–13"),
    ("4. Revelation 19 announces the marriage of the Lamb to the wife of the prophets, and Revelation 21 names her city by the names of Israel’s tribes.", "Revelation 19:7–9; 21:2, 9–14; 22:17"),
    ("5. The Body of Christ is not the bride. The Body has its own figures, and the mystery of the Body is not found in the prophets.", "Ephesians 1:22–23; 2:15; 3:3–9; Colossians 1:26–27"),
    ("6. Paul’s uses of marriage teach the Body how to love and how to be faithful. They do not transfer the wife’s identity or her covenant.", "Ephesians 5:22–33; 2 Corinthians 11:2–3; Romans 7:1–4"),
  ], [0.62, 0.38]),
("p", "Two principles govern every identification in the volume. The first is that a name, a covenant or a promise which Scripture gives to one people is not moved to another people because the same words appear in both places. This volume calls it the principle of no transfer. Shared words are a reason to compare the passages. They are not a reason to merge the people. The second is that the Body of Christ is a company formed under the gospel Paul calls the mystery, and that it is neither Israel nor the remnant of Israel, and it has no part in the covenants of Israel. Where the two principles are applied, the reader is shown the evidence from the text and not only the conclusion."),
("p", "The method is plain. For each figure the volume sets out the passages that speak of it, notes who is addressed and what is said of them, follows the same figure through the later passages, and asks whether any passage gives the figure to a different people. If a passage does, the volume says so and reads it in its own setting. If none does, the volume does not supply what the text does not say. The result is a reading that can be tested: a reader may take any claim in the table above and look for the verse that overturns it."),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "The prophets give the LORD the title of husband and Israel the title of wife, and they promise the wife’s restoration. Revelation completes that promise and names the wife’s city by Israel’s tribes. The Body of Christ is joined to Christ as a body to its head, and Paul teaches it through the pattern of marriage without calling it the bride."),
]
